<!-- AREAS -->
<section style="padding:76px 24px 60px;background:#fff;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;text-align:center;">
    <span class="kicker">Service Areas</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3vw,36px);margin:0 0 16px;">Serving Adelaide</h2>
    <div class="rich" style="text-align:left;"><p>Torrens Survey Co covers Greater Adelaide and the Adelaide Hills. Pick your part of town: <a href="/surveyors-adelaide-hills/">Adelaide Hills</a>, <a href="/surveyors-northern-adelaide/">Northern Adelaide</a>, <a href="/surveyors-southern-adelaide/">Southern Adelaide</a>, <a href="/surveyors-eastern-suburbs/">Eastern Suburbs</a>, <a href="/surveyors-western-suburbs/">Western Suburbs</a> and <a href="/surveyors-inner-adelaide/">Inner Adelaide</a>. Each page sets out what the Greater Adelaide Regional Plan says about that part of the city and how the four survey types apply there.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EEF3F1;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need a boundary survey, or a different kind of survey?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">It depends on the problem. A boundary survey (also called an identification survey or re-pegging) is for when you need the legal boundary marked, for example because of a fence dispute, a build close to the line or a purchase. A feature and level survey records the existing ground levels and features so a designer can draw plans. A land division survey is part of splitting a block. If you're not sure, call (08) 5550 3814 and describe the situation, and we will point you to the right one.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Who is allowed to do a boundary survey in South Australia?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">The Surveyors Board of South Australia says that in South Australia, only licensed surveyors can undertake a boundary survey or place a survey mark that defines a property boundary. Torrens Survey Co isn't one of them: it's an advertising and introduction service and holds no surveying licence. Check any surveyor's licence with the Registrar at the Surveyors Board (registrar@isasa.org.au or (08) 8212 0343) before you agree to work.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">How much does a boundary survey or land survey cost in Adelaide, and how is the job scoped?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">Surveyors quote each job, and the price depends on the block, the evidence on site and the type of survey. None of the Adelaide firms we checked publishes a price for a boundary or feature survey, so this site doesn't either. The one sourced figure is a whole-of-project estimate for a standard one-into-two land division in metropolitan Adelaide, from one Adelaide surveying firm's published guide (May 2025): 'in the order of $30,000 – $34,000', with GST treatment not stated. That covers government fees, conveyancing and other costs as well as the surveyor, so it's not a survey price. Ask the surveyor for the scope in writing: what's measured, what you receive and what's not included. Our cost page sets out what is and isn't sourced.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">How quickly can I get a survey done?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">We don't publish a lead time. We have no sourced figure for one, and it would be a guess. How soon a surveyor can start depends on their workload and the type of survey, so ask for a start date and a delivery date when they quote. A land division has approvals on top of the survey, and Land Services SA says the division of land can take many months, sometimes even years. If your timing matters, say so when you call (08) 5550 3814.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">My neighbour and I disagree about where the fence should go. What now?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">SA Government says the boundary can only be determined by a licensed surveyor, and a fence line doesn't show the legal boundary. Under the Fences Act 1975, the cost of a survey reasonably required for the fencing work can be included as a fencing cost, though your neighbour may object and a Magistrate would then decide. A neighbour has 30 days to respond to a fencing notice. By law you can't move or remove a survey mark, and for the dispute itself, talk to the Legal Services Commission, Community Mediation Services or a solicitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 28px;box-shadow:0 3px 12px rgba(10,61,92,0.06);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Instrument Sans',sans-serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:700;color:#111418;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I split my block, and where does the survey fit?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#5A6B76;margin:0;">We can't tell you whether a division will be approved. That depends on your zone, the overlays and the council, so a preliminary enquiry at the council is the first step, and this site doesn't publish minimum block sizes. Land Services SA strongly recommends using a professional licensed surveyor and a conveyancer or solicitor to work out the planning requirements, draft the plans and lodge the paperwork. Our land division page and the blog on how to subdivide land in South Australia cover the steps in order.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

